---
/**
 * Base layout — the HTML shell every page renders through.
 * Handles document head: title/description (props), canonical + Open Graph /
 * Twitter metadata, theme-color for both color schemes, favicons, self-hosted
 * font preloads, and the token → fonts → global stylesheet cascade.
 *
 * Client-side JS: one script, the pre-paint theme script below (ADR-0007).
 * The other sanctioned script — the ADR-0005 reveal observer — lives in
 * src/pages/index.astro. Two inline scripts site-wide, no others, no
 * libraries (OVERVIEW.md §3.1, §3.4 as amended by ADR-0007).
 *
 * --- The manual scheme toggle (phase-2-ws-2.6, ADR-0007) -----------------
 *
 * tokens.css owns the cascade half of this feature: dark on `:root`, light in
 * a `prefers-color-scheme: light` media query, and two `:root[data-theme=…]`
 * blocks at higher specificity that let an explicit choice beat the OS in
 * both directions. This file owns the other half — the state, and the control
 * that sets it.
 *
 * The script, expanded (it is written hand-minified on one line below because
 * `is:inline` content is passed through verbatim and never minified):
 *
 *   (() => {
 *     var d = document, r = d.documentElement,
 *         K = "theme", T = "data-theme", Q = "[data-theme-toggle]",
 *         m = matchMedia("(prefers-color-scheme:light)"),
 *         ok = 1, stored;
 *     // Effective scheme: the explicit choice if there is one, else the OS.
 *     var cur = () => r.getAttribute(T) || (m.matches ? "light" : "dark");
 *     // Reveal the control and put its state in sync with the scheme.
 *     var sync = () => {
 *       var b = d.querySelector(Q);
 *       if (b && ok) {
 *         var light = cur() == "light";
 *         b.hidden = false;
 *         b.setAttribute("aria-pressed", light);
 *         b.setAttribute("aria-label",
 *           light ? "Switch to dark theme" : "Switch to light theme");
 *       }
 *     };
 *     try { stored = localStorage.getItem(K); } catch (e) { ok = 0 }
 *     if (stored == "light" || stored == "dark") r.setAttribute(T, stored);
 *     d.addEventListener("click", (e) => {
 *       if (e.target.closest?.(Q)) {
 *         var next = cur() == "light" ? "dark" : "light";
 *         r.setAttribute(T, next);
 *         try { localStorage.setItem(K, next) } catch (e) { r.removeAttribute(T) }
 *         sync();
 *       }
 *     });
 *     d.addEventListener("DOMContentLoaded", sync);
 *     m.addEventListener("change", sync);
 *   })();
 *
 * Why it is shaped this way:
 *
 *   - It runs in <head>, before the stylesheet link Astro appends at the end
 *     of <head>, so `data-theme` is on <html> before the first paint. That
 *     ordering is the whole point: read it late and the visitor sees a flash
 *     of the OS scheme before their choice applies.
 *   - Only the two literals `"light"` and `"dark"` are honored; a hand-edited
 *     or stale value leaves the attribute unset, i.e. OS behavior.
 *   - The click listener is delegated on `document` so it can be attached
 *     from <head>, before the button it serves exists. `closest?.` guards the
 *     case where the event target is not an Element.
 *   - `sync()` runs on DOMContentLoaded because the button is not parsed yet
 *     when this script executes. It is also what un-hides the button: the
 *     control ships with `hidden`, so a no-JS visitor never sees a dead one.
 *   - Both accessible labels appear as whole literals rather than being
 *     concatenated, so each is greppable verbatim in the built HTML against
 *     docs/copy.md §ThemeToggle.
 *
 * --- Storage failure and live OS changes (review cycle 1, f-014) ---------
 *
 * ADR-0007 requires that a thrown localStorage access degrade to
 * OS-preference behavior, never to a broken page, and the same ADR's
 * progressive-enhancement rule is that a visitor never sees a dead control.
 * Both clauses bind at once, so storage failure is handled in two places:
 *
 *   - Read throws (private mode, storage blocked): `ok` goes to 0 and
 *     `sync()` never un-hides the button. The visitor keeps pure
 *     `prefers-color-scheme` behavior and is not offered a switch that
 *     cannot hold its answer. The read is the capability probe — probing
 *     with a scratch write is not available, because a second localStorage
 *     key would break the one-key acceptance criterion.
 *   - Write throws anyway (quota, and only reachable when the read worked):
 *     `data-theme` is removed, so the effective scheme falls straight back
 *     to the OS. Set and removed inside one task, so no paint happens in
 *     between and the revert cannot flash.
 *
 * The `change` listener on the media query is the other half of f-014.
 * Without it, a visitor with no stored override who flips their OS scheme
 * live got the repainted page (that is pure CSS) but a stale `aria-pressed`
 * and a stale `aria-label` — announcing the exact opposite of what the next
 * click would do. `sync()` recomputes from `cur()`, so the listener is a
 * no-op while an override is set and corrects the label when one is not.
 *
 * Both fixes were paid for out of the same budget rather than added to it:
 * hoisting the three repeated string literals (`T`, `Q`, `m`) and switching
 * to arrow functions freed more bytes than the new behavior costs, so the
 * script is smaller after the fix than before it.
 *   - `aria-pressed` is defined as "the light scheme is active". Pairing a
 *     pressed state with an action-phrased label is an acknowledged ARIA
 *     tension (a screen reader says "Switch to dark theme, pressed"), but
 *     ADR-0007 mandates syncing `aria-pressed` and the label wording is fixed
 *     by the copy deck; one consistent definition is the best available
 *     reading, and it is stated here so it is not re-litigated per review.
 *   - `<meta name="theme-color">` stays media-query-driven. Under a manual
 *     override browser chrome may follow the OS instead — accepted in
 *     ADR-0007's consequences rather than paid for out of the script budget.
 *
 * Budget: ≤ 768 bytes in dist (ADR-0007 gate), measured the way the phase-1
 * gate measured the observer — inner text and whole element both reported.
 */
import { SITE, THEME_COLOR } from "../consts";

// Import order is load order: tokens define the fallback font stacks, fonts
// enriches them with the real faces, global consumes the resulting tokens.
import "../styles/tokens.css";
import "../styles/fonts.css";
import "../styles/global.css";

interface Props {
  /** Document title — appears in <title> and og:title. */
  title: string;
  /** Meta description — appears in <meta name="description"> and og:description. */
  description: string;
}

const { title, description } = Astro.props;

/**
 * Join a path in public/ onto the configured base path (phase-2-ws-2.3,
 * ADR-0006). Astro rebases root-absolute `url()` in CSS during the build but
 * passes template attribute values through verbatim, so every hand-written
 * asset href in this file goes through here.
 *
 * `BASE_URL` is "/metiswww" for the current config and "/" with no base, but
 * Astro normalizes it against `trailingSlash` — so the join trims and
 * re-adds exactly one slash rather than assuming either form.
 */
const asset = (path: string) =>
  `${import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, "")}/${path.replace(/^\//, "")}`;

// Resolve the page path against SITE.url rather than concatenating onto it.
// `Astro.url.pathname` already carries the base ("/metiswww/"), so resolution
// preserves the project segment via the path; concatenating would emit it
// twice (the defect phase-2-ws-2.2 measured and handed to this slice). Only
// SITE.url's origin reaches the output, which is what makes the double-count
// unreachable rather than merely fixed.
const pageUrl = new URL(Astro.url.pathname, SITE.url);
// Absolute, and used for BOTH og:url and rel="canonical" (phase-2-ws-2.2).
// og:url has always required it (Open Graph spec). The canonical link was
// root-relative until this slice — valid HTML that resolves to the same URL,
// chosen in phase-0-ws-0.2 to keep the built HTML free of any
// <link href="http…">, which the phase-0 gate greps for. Lighthouse scores a
// relative canonical as no canonical at all ("Is not an absolute URL"), the
// one thing standing between this page and SEO 100, and Google's
// canonicalization rules agree. The gate criterion behind that grep is about
// external *resources* — "no external origins for scripts, stylesheets, or
// fonts", "no request to any external origin on page load" — and a canonical
// link fetches nothing, exactly like the og:url <meta> that already carries
// this same absolute URL. So the grep gains one permitted hit and the
// invariant it stands for is untouched; the brief records the refinement.
const canonical = pageUrl.href;
---

<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <meta charset="utf-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1" />

    <!-- Pre-paint theme state (ADR-0007) — rationale in the frontmatter above.
         Placed here so it runs before the stylesheet Astro appends at the end
         of <head>: the attribute must be on <html> before the first paint. -->
    <script is:inline>(()=>{var d=document,r=d.documentElement,K="theme",T="data-theme",Q="[data-theme-toggle]",m=matchMedia("(prefers-color-scheme:light)"),o=1,t,c=()=>r.getAttribute(T)||(m.matches?"light":"dark"),s=()=>{var b=d.querySelector(Q);if(b&&o){var l=c()=="light";b.hidden=!1;b.setAttribute("aria-pressed",l);b.setAttribute("aria-label",l?"Switch to dark theme":"Switch to light theme")}};try{t=localStorage.getItem(K)}catch(e){o=0}if(t=="light"||t=="dark")r.setAttribute(T,t);d.addEventListener("click",e=>{if(e.target.closest?.(Q)){var n=c()=="light"?"dark":"light";r.setAttribute(T,n);try{localStorage.setItem(K,n)}catch(e){r.removeAttribute(T)}s()}});d.addEventListener("DOMContentLoaded",s);m.addEventListener("change",s)})()</script>

    <title>{title}</title>
    <meta name="description" content={description} />
    <link rel="canonical" href={canonical} />

    <!-- Open Graph -->
    <meta property="og:title" content={title} />
    <meta property="og:description" content={description} />
    <meta property="og:type" content="website" />
    <meta property="og:url" content={canonical} />

    <!-- Twitter -->
    <meta name="twitter:card" content="summary" />
    <meta name="twitter:title" content={title} />
    <meta name="twitter:description" content={description} />

    <!-- Browser UI theme color, per scheme (values mirror tokens.css) -->
    <meta
      name="theme-color"
      media="(prefers-color-scheme: dark)"
      content={THEME_COLOR.dark}
    />
    <meta
      name="theme-color"
      media="(prefers-color-scheme: light)"
      content={THEME_COLOR.light}
    />

    <!-- Favicons (base-path aware — see asset() above) -->
    <link rel="icon" type="image/svg+xml" href={asset("favicon.svg")} />
    <link rel="icon" href={asset("favicon.ico")} sizes="any" />

    <!-- Preload self-hosted variable fonts (same-origin; crossorigin required
         for font fetches even when same-origin). These must match the URLs
         fonts.css requests or the preload is wasted — the build rebases the
         stylesheet's url(), asset() rebases these. -->
    <link
      rel="preload"
      href={asset("fonts/space-grotesk-variable.woff2")}
      as="font"
      type="font/woff2"
      crossorigin
    />
    <link
      rel="preload"
      href={asset("fonts/jetbrains-mono-variable.woff2")}
      as="font"
      type="font/woff2"
      crossorigin
    />

    <meta name="generator" content={Astro.generator} />
  </head>
  <body>
    <div class="theme-bar">
      <button
        type="button"
        class="theme-toggle"
        data-theme-toggle
        aria-pressed="false"
        aria-label="Switch to light theme"
        hidden></button>
    </div>
    <slot />
  </body>
</html>

<style>
  /* --- Manual scheme toggle (phase-2-ws-2.6, ADR-0007) --------------------

     ADR-0007 requires "no layout shift when the button un-hides — reserve its
     box". The bar is taken out of flow instead, which is the stronger
     guarantee of the same property: an absolutely positioned box contributes
     nothing to layout at any point, so there is no shift to reserve against
     whether the button is hidden, revealed, or never revealed at all (no-JS).
     Reserving an in-flow box would have met the rule too, at the cost of
     ~48px of dead space above the hero for every visitor including the ones
     who never get the control.

     The containing block is the initial one (nothing between here and the
     root establishes another), so the bar is anchored to the top of the
     document and scrolls away with the hero — the "top-of-page toggle" of
     OVERVIEW §3.2. Its own box repeats index.astro's container rules so the
     button lines up with the right edge of the page's content column instead
     of the viewport.

     Deliberately not wrapped in <header>: that would add a `banner` landmark
     the page does not otherwise have, which is outside this slice's brief and
     would be an empty landmark for no-JS visitors. The button is the only
     content out here and it is hidden until scripted.

     It sits above the hero's --space-8 top padding, so it cannot overlap the
     headline; `pointer-events` keeps the bar's full-width box from
     intercepting anything that passes under it regardless. */
  .theme-bar {
    position: absolute;
    inset-block-start: var(--space-4);
    inset-inline: 0;
    max-inline-size: var(--container-max);
    margin-inline: auto;
    padding-inline: var(--space-4);
    display: flex;
    justify-content: flex-end;
    pointer-events: none;
  }

  /* 32px box — above the 24px WCAG 2.5.8 target minimum. No visible text
     label: the affordance is the two-tone disc sanctioned by docs/copy.md
     §ThemeToggle, and the accessible name is carried by aria-label. */
  .theme-toggle {
    pointer-events: auto;
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
    inline-size: var(--space-5);
    block-size: var(--space-5);
    padding: var(--space-0);
    border: none;
    border-radius: 50%;
    background: none;
    cursor: pointer;
  }

  /* `hidden` is the no-JS state and must survive the display rule above. */
  .theme-toggle[hidden] {
    display: none;
  }

  /* The disc: a ring, half filled. It needs no aria-hiding — a pseudo-element
     has no accessibility presence. --color-muted against --color-bg is
     8.39:1 dark / 5.66:1 light (tokens.css ratio table), comfortably past the
     3:1 AA minimum for non-text UI, and it inverts with the scheme, which is
     the feedback for the press. */
  .theme-toggle::before {
    content: "";
    inline-size: var(--space-4);
    block-size: var(--space-4);
    border: 1px solid var(--color-muted);
    border-radius: 50%;
    background-image: linear-gradient(
      to right,
      var(--color-muted) 0 50%,
      transparent 50% 100%
    );
    transition: border-color var(--duration-fast) var(--ease-out);
  }

  .theme-toggle:hover::before {
    border-color: var(--color-text);
  }

  /* global.css rounds every focus ring to --radius; on a circular control
     that reads as a mistake. Same properties, matching shape. */
  .theme-toggle:focus-visible {
    border-radius: 50%;
  }
</style>
